# Dart Style Guide

## Naming Conventions
- Classes: `UpperCamelCase` (e.g., `ChatScreen`, `MessageWidget`)
- Variables/Functions: `lowerCamelCase` (e.g., `userName`, `sendMessage()`)
- Constants: `lowerCamelCase` (e.g., `defaultTimeout`)
- Private: prefix with `_` (e.g., `_privateMethod()`)
- Files: `snake_case` (e.g., `chat_screen.dart`)

## Import Order
1. Dart SDK imports (`dart:`)
2. Flutter imports (`package:flutter/`)
3. Third-party packages (`package:`)
4. Local imports (relative paths)

```dart
import 'dart:async';

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

import 'package:firebase_ai/firebase_ai.dart';
import 'package:flutter_ai_toolkit/flutter_ai_toolkit.dart';

import '../widgets/chat_widget.dart';
```

## Widget Best Practices
```dart
// Prefer const constructors — Flutter's primary rebuild optimization
const MyWidget({super.key});
const EdgeInsets.all(16);
const SizedBox(height: 8);

// Use named parameters
Widget build({
  required String title,
  String? subtitle,
  VoidCallback? onTap,
});

// Prefer independent StatelessWidget for stable, self-contained subtrees
// (enables widget identity + const; better than returning from a method)
class _SectionHeader extends StatelessWidget {
  const _SectionHeader({required this.title});
  final String title;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Text(title, style: ...);
}

// Use _buildXxx() helper methods only when the subtree reads parent State directly
Widget _buildContent() {
  return Text(_controller.text);  // needs access to _controller
}
```

## Async/Await
```dart
// Always handle errors
Future<void> loadData() async {
  try {
    final result = await api.fetchData();
    if (!mounted) return;  // check mounted after every await
    setState(() => data = result);
  } catch (e) {
    debugPrint('Error: $e');
  }
}

// Dialog awaits require mounted checks too — most commonly missed
onTap: () async {
  final picked = await showTimePicker(context: context, initialTime: TimeOfDay.now());
  if (!mounted) return;  // widget may have unmounted while dialog was open
  if (picked != null) onChanged(picked);
},
```

## Documentation
- Use `///` for public API documentation
- Keep comments concise and meaningful
- Document complex logic, not obvious code
